WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.400
What really happened to Albert Wesker? Are the&nbsp;
Ashford twins more than an Easter egg? Does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.160
Chief Irons know more than he's&nbsp;
letting on? All that and more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.360
right here. Spoilers ahead!
 &nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:19.200
The "Resident Evil" film saga gets a reboot with&nbsp;
2021's "Resident Evil: Welcome to Raccoon City."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.680
Skewing more closely to Capcom's original&nbsp;
video games than the previous film series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.720
this new spin on the Umbrella Corporation's&nbsp;
ghastly experiments with bio-organic weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.880
takes players back to the beginning.
"Resident Evil: Welcome to Raccoon City"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.080
opens with the brother-sister duo of Chris and&nbsp;
Claire Redfield as children in the Raccoon City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.600
Orphanage. Claire is visited by Lisa Trevor,&nbsp;
a tragic monster whose backstory is unexplored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.240
in this movie. Unlike in the 2002 remake&nbsp;
of the original "Resident Evil," however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.920
Lisa is not a raging monster, but a scared&nbsp;
little girl who, despite her ghastly visage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.440
only wants to be Claire's friend. The true villain&nbsp;
of the intro and, indeed, the whole film, is Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.600
William Birkin, who uses the orphanage as a source&nbsp;
of fresh meat for testing his viral concoctions.&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.280
As in "Resident Evil 2," Birkin is shot by an&nbsp;
agent looking to secure a sample of the G-Virus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.840
He survives by injecting himself with&nbsp;
the experimental serum and transforming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.280
into a monster. His first form is taken down&nbsp;
by Chris and Claire's well-placed shotgun slugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.240
and a point-blank handgun headshot. Like any&nbsp;
classic "Resident Evil" final boss, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.840
he returns in an even more mutated state. He&nbsp;
nearly kills the Redfield siblings before Leon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.800
shows up with a rocket launcher and takes him&nbsp;
out for good with a missile to the face. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.360
is most definitely a nod to the video games,&nbsp;
where this kind of thing happens all the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.320
Having a rocket launcher appear in the film's&nbsp;
climax is a true gift to fans of the games.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.360
"You figured out the one thing I wanted more than anything else in the entire world and you got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.840
it for me, and it's perfect."

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.640
One of the biggest divergences from the games in&nbsp;
"Resident Evil: Welcome to Raccoon City" comes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.600
the form of the leader of the doomed S.T.A.R.S.&nbsp;
team sent to investigate the Spencer Mansion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.160
What's his name? Oh, yeah:
 
"Albert Wesker."
 &nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.200
In the game, Wesker is a double agent who has been&nbsp;
working for the Umbrella Corporation all along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.760
helping to create their bio-organic weapons&nbsp;
and basically causing all kinds of problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.960
and being a jerk to the heroes. 
 
"You guys are idiots."
 &nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.240
In the movie, he has no loyalty to&nbsp;
Umbrella, yet he still betrays his team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.040
This time, it's out of financial desperation&nbsp;
and a desire to leave Raccoon City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.000
a corporate town that would be seen as dying&nbsp;
even if the entire population wasn't turning into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.640
flesh-eating zombies. He is given a Palm Pilot&nbsp;
with a map of the mansion and instructions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.760
retrieve a sample of the G-Virus. This puts&nbsp;
him on a crash course with Dr. William Birkin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.960
which doesn't end well for either of them.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.080
As in "Resident Evil 2," "Resident Evil: Welcome&nbsp;
to Raccoon City" ends with the heroes and young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.040
Sherry Birkin on an Umbrella cargo train, leaving&nbsp;
the city. But in the original "Resident Evil,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.000
they escape via helicopter thanks to S.T.A.R.S.&nbsp;
pilot Brad Vickers, who rescues them from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.080
roof of the mansion after throwing down the&nbsp;
rocket launcher they use to defeat the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.240
Tyrant. Brad Vickers does show up in the&nbsp;
movie, but he doesn't make it past the mansion:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.360
He's ambushed by zombies, flies his helicopter&nbsp;
into the mansion, and dies in a massive explosion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.640
that nearly kills Jill and Wesker. Between the&nbsp;
train escape and what was almost a helicopter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.120
rescue, "Welcome to Raccoon City" manages to&nbsp;
nod to both of the games that inspired it.
 &nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.920
The fate of Raccoon City

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.760
original games or Paul W.S. Anderson's films,&nbsp;
the fate of Raccoon City is always bleak.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.880
In Resident Evil 3, the city is wiped off the&nbsp;
map by a missile strike, which contains the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.760
outbreak at a terrible cost. In "Resident Evil:&nbsp;
Welcome to Raccoon City," the destruction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.400
Raccoon City is portrayed somewhat differently.&nbsp;
Rather than a tactical strike from outside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.160
city limits, Raccoon City is essentially made to&nbsp;
self-destruct through an underground detonation.&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.960
Apparently, Umbrella has had the entire city&nbsp;
rigged to implode in the event of an outbreak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.920
as a fail-safe system to contain any prospective&nbsp;
infection. While some fans might be upset to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.760
see this different version of Raccoon City's&nbsp;
destruction, it's worth noting that "Resident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.480
Evil: Apocalypse," while often ridiculed by&nbsp;
gamers, does feature a faithful recreation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.880
of the missile strike shown in "Resident Evil&nbsp;
3." So sure, it makes sense for the new movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.200
to try something different.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.840
After "Resident Evil: Welcome to Raccoon City"&nbsp;
ends, a brief scene interrupts the credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.760
showing the return of Albert Wesker. No,&nbsp;
he didn't survive being shot to death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.960
by Jill Valentine -- he's been revived by the&nbsp;
mysterious benefactor who ordered him to steal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.600
Umbrella's secrets. This person reveals&nbsp;
herself to be none other than Ada Wong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.600
the fan-favorite spy who made her&nbsp;
debut appearance in "Resident Evil 2."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.960
She explains that she brought Wesker back&nbsp;
to life, and hands him a pair of sunglasses.&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.200
In the games, Wesker is almost never seen&nbsp;
without his iconic sunglasses -- he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.189
the Rex Kramer of the video game world.

00:04:55.189 --> 00:04:57.280
"Alright, Steve, let's face a few facts."

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.480
The post-credits scene works as something of&nbsp;
an origin story for this new take on one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.760
most popular villains in video game history. It's&nbsp;
also worth noting that this Wesker and Ada occupy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.720
roles that are the opposite of the ones they&nbsp;
hold in the games. There, Ada works for Wesker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.840
during the Raccoon City Incident, as shown in the&nbsp;
"Death's Door" scenario of 2007's "Resident Evil:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.880
The Umbrella Chronicles." By the time the&nbsp;
events of 2005's "Resident Evil 4" roll around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.000
Ada is ostensibly aligned with Wesker, but&nbsp;
secretly working with a still-unknown employer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.440
In "Resident Evil: Welcome to Raccoon City,"&nbsp;
Wesker is basically a regular cop Ada introduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.840
to a new world of espionage and intrigue.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.040
In the wake of Raccoon City's destruction, William&nbsp;
Birkin's death, and Albert Wesker's resurrection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.680
unanswered questions emerge. Firstly,&nbsp;
does Lisa Trevor survive? In the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.240
she's nigh invincible, only dying for good&nbsp;
when the mansion explodes around her. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.040
"Be a good girl and stay dead this time."
 
In the film, she shows off her physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.720
strength when she tears a Licker&nbsp;
apart in physical combat. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.680
she and her video game counterpart clearly have&nbsp;
physical traits in common. Much of her game-bound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.640
backstory doesn't appear in the film, however.&nbsp;
Will fans learn more in a subsequent film?&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.960
Secondly, who is Ada Wong working for?&nbsp;
In both the games and "Resident Evil:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.840
Welcome to Raccoon City," Ada Wong is a&nbsp;
mysterious agent with a penchant for spy games. 
 &nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.400
"I was just some pawn to you?"
 
"Look, I'm just doing my job."
 &nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.200
It's a sure bet she'll play a key role in&nbsp;
a sequel, if the box office fates allow it.&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.240
Thirdly, how much does Chief Irons know&nbsp;
about Umbrella? In the games, Brian Irons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:39.920
the RPD's chief of police, is an outright villain&nbsp;
who murders the mayor's daughter and nearly kills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.920
Sherry Birkin. In the movie, he seems to&nbsp;
know about Umbrella's dastardly deeds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:49.120
but he's not a villain -- in fact, he even brings&nbsp;
Claire and Leon to the Raccoon City Orphanage and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:54.080
tells them how to get to Spencer Mansion before&nbsp;
getting attacked and killed by a Licker. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:58.800
likely in on Umbrella's plot to some degree, but&nbsp;
whether or not that qualifies him as an outright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.760
bad guy is open to debate.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:06.480
Traditionally, each game in the "Resident Evil"&nbsp;
saga tells its own story, set within a wider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.200
interconnected world. Sometimes, characters&nbsp;
and storylines are dropped for years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.000
being picked up again. For example, Sherry&nbsp;
Birkin from 1998's "Resident Evil 2" doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.360
make another proper, canonical appearance&nbsp;
until 2012's "Resident Evil 6," where she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.320
appears as one of the main playable characters.
The point is, with characters coming in and out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:31.280
of the games all the time, a film sequel could&nbsp;
conceivably go anywhere. In "Resident Evil:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:35.360
Welcome to Raccoon City," the story wraps&nbsp;
up in a reasonably airtight fashion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.360
meaning the filmmakers have their options open&nbsp;
when it comes to how to approach a sequel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.240
Teasing the Ashford twins might be more than&nbsp;
just an Easter egg: A second movie could adapt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:48.720
portions of 2000's "Resident Evil -- Code:&nbsp;
Veronica," which takes Chris and Claire to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:54.400
an Antarctic base where they fight the Ashfords&nbsp;
and a powered-up Wesker. In the games' canon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:58.000
Jill's next key appearance following&nbsp;
the destruction of Raccoon City occurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:03.200
in "Resident Evil 5," where she is brainwashed&nbsp;
by Wesker to fight against her old allies. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:09.680
"Still resisting at such an advanced&nbsp;
stage. Commendable, yet futile."

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.720
Leon next appears in the&nbsp;
beloved "Resident Evil 4."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.640
Though it's tonally different from the prior&nbsp;
games, it wouldn't be surprising to see some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:22.560
variation of that entry's "Leon in Spain" premise&nbsp;
form the basis of a future "Resident Evil" movie.&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.800
Finally, while the Umbrella Corporation is&nbsp;
the main antagonist of the film -- and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:31.680
"Resident Evil" projects -- it's mostly absent&nbsp;
from "Resident Evil: Welcome to Raccoon City."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.560
However, the film's ending suggests they will&nbsp;
continue to be the villains of future projects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.440
If so, Ozwell Spencer, the founder of Umbrella&nbsp;
-- who is mentioned by name in the film -- will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:46.320
likely make an appearance. As he has a close&nbsp;
relationship with Albert Wesker in the games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.320
this new film series could do something&nbsp;
interesting with their particular dynamic.

